## H2 — stmt `println` heap vs sibling `eprintln`

### Mechanism (two walkers, both already sliced/token)

| Rail | File | Extract | Concat? |
|------|------|---------|---------|
| check / llvm front-door | `c_emit_secret.oo` L175–193 | `field_at(..., 3)` then `c_secret_refuse_sink_resolved` | **none** (token text already paid) |
| stmt `println` emit | `c_emit_ident.oo` L129–131 → `c_emit_print.oo` L9–85 | same IDENT field + `"__sec__" + sn` | **no ident rebuild**; no join |
| free-call `eprintln` / `f(...)` | `c_emit_lower.oo` L90 → `c_secret_emit_guard` | `c_guard_scan_idents` **one `str_slice` per ident** (`s71_memory.oot` PASS) | join is **`c_emit_call_args` L40** (pre-existing, all free-calls) |

Stmt `println` **never** builds a joined C `args` string. It lowers each arg via `c_emit_expr_c` and prints `oo_print_*` + `oo_println()`. `eprintln` **does** join (`eprintln(a0000, a0001, …)` in emit-c) then scans that text.

`oo_char_at` is still Θ(n²) **time** + 1-char temps (compiler-wide). Host `oodac` is the **07:49** ELF (same as s61/s71/s72/s710/s28).

